# Build context for deploy/node/Dockerfile.
#
# BuildKit reads `<dockerfile>.dockerignore` in preference to the context root's `.dockerignore`
# (same mechanism deploy/coordinator/Dockerfile.dockerignore uses). Whitelist approach: exclude
# everything, then name back in exactly what the Dockerfile's stages COPY -- the mesh workspace,
# the three .NET subtrees' *source* (not their local build output, which the container rebuilds
# fresh), the fetched third-party binaries for the platforms this image ships, the web bundle, and
# the top-level license files. Everything else in this monorepo (apps/stingstream's own
# node_modules/android/ios, the other four vendored subtrees, tools/, docs/) would otherwise make
# the context many gigabytes for no benefit -- none of it reaches the final image.

*

!mesh/Cargo.toml
!mesh/Cargo.lock
!mesh/crates/**
mesh/target/
**/target/
mesh/jellyswarrm/

!server/jellyfin/**
!server/radarr/**
!server/sonarr/**
# Local build/publish litter from a developer's own out-of-container builds -- irrelevant here and
# occasionally the wrong platform's binaries, which would otherwise get copied into the context and
# ignored, but there is no reason to send them over the build socket at all.
**/bin/
**/obj/
**/_output/
**/_tests/
**/_temp/
**/node_modules/

!third_party/ffmpeg/bin/**
!third_party/nzbget/bin/**
# The archives fetch-jellyfin-ffmpeg.ps1 leaves alongside the extracted binaries, and the
# installer/uninstaller nzbget's own Windows fetch leaves (irrelevant to the Linux platforms this
# image ships, and the Dockerfile's `thirdparty` stage does not look for them).
third_party/ffmpeg/bin/*/*.zip
third_party/ffmpeg/bin/*/*.tar.xz
third_party/ffmpeg/bin/*/*.tar.gz
third_party/nzbget/bin/*/*-setup.exe
third_party/nzbget/bin/*/*.run
third_party/nzbget/bin/*/Uninstall.exe

!apps/stingstream/dist/**

!LICENSE
!NOTICE.md
